\begin{abstract}
The First Atoms Engine (FAE) is a conceptual propulsion architecture based on
engineered atomic energy-density gradients. Motion arises not from reaction
mass or spacetime curvature, but from drift through stability-biased atomic
fields generated by external astrophysical inputs and internal fusion anchors.
This paper formalizes the atomic field framework, motion law, simulation
kernel, and relativistic constraints governing FAE. While not physically
realizable with current technology, FAE provides a coherent theoretical model
for atomic-state propulsion.
\end{abstract}

\section*{Keywords}
Atomic stability fields; gradient propulsion; fusion anchors; entropy
reinforcement; relativistic drift; conceptual propulsion architecture.

\section{Introduction}
Atomic-gradient propulsion represents a theoretical alternative to classical
thrust systems. The First Atoms Engine (FAE) proposes that motion can emerge
from engineered differences in atomic energy-density fields. By coupling an
internal fusion anchor to an externally defined stability field, FAE produces a
gradient that induces drift without exhaust. This paper presents the formal
architecture and simulation behavior of FAE.

\section{Atomic Stability Fields}
FAE constructs two atomic stability fields:
\begin{equation}
    \Phi_{A,ext}(t,\mathbf{x}) =
    f\big(\mathcal{C}(t),\mathcal{S}(t,\lambda),Z_{env},\mathbf{x}\big)
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    \Phi_{A,int}(t,\mathbf{x}) =
    g\big(\mathbf{F}_{Pt/U}(t),\mathbf{M}_{conf}(t),\Psi_{core}(t),\mathbf{x}\big)
\end{equation}
The atomic stability gradient is:
\begin{equation}
    \nabla \Phi_{A}(t,\mathbf{x}) =
    \Phi_{A,ext}(t,\mathbf{x}) - \Phi_{A,int}(t,\mathbf{x})
\end{equation}

\section{Entropy Reinforcement}
Fusion energy spikes contribute to atomic-scale entropy:
\begin{equation}
    S_{A}(t) = k_{S} \cdot E_{spike}(t)
\end{equation}
The reinforced gradient is:
\begin{equation}
    \nabla \Phi_{A}^{*}(t,\mathbf{x}) =
    \nabla \Phi_{A}(t,\mathbf{x}) \cdot
    \big(1 + \alpha_{S} S_{A}(t)\big)
\end{equation}

\section{Motion Law}
The effective drift velocity is:
\begin{equation}
    v_{eff}(t,\mathbf{x}) =
    k_{A} \cdot \left|\nabla \Phi_{A}^{*}(t,\mathbf{x})\right| \cdot S_{A}(t)
\end{equation}
with relativistic constraint:
\begin{equation}
    v_{eff}(t,\mathbf{x}) < c
\end{equation}
Acceleration is:
\begin{equation}
    a_{eff}(t,\mathbf{x}) =
    \frac{\partial v_{eff}(t,\mathbf{x})}{\partial t}
\end{equation}

\section{Simulation Kernel}
The FAE kernel evolves five state sets:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\text{EnvState}(t)$
    \item $\text{AnchorState}(t)$
    \item $\text{FieldState}(t)$
    \item $\text{EntropyState}(t)$
    \item $\text{DriftState}(t)$
\end{itemize}

At each timestep:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Construct $\Phi_{A,ext}$ and $\Phi_{A,int}$
    \item Solve $\nabla \Phi_{A}$
    \item Compute $S_{A}(t)$
    \item Reinforce gradient $\nabla \Phi_{A}^{*}$
    \item Evaluate $v_{eff}$ and $a_{eff}$
\end{enumerate}

\section{Results}
A conceptual simulation over $10{,}000$ seconds shows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Early micro-drift (Bands I--II)
    \item Mid-phase acceleration with increasing entropy (Band III)
    \item Late-phase relativistic drift approaching a fraction of $c$ (Band IV)
\end{itemize}
Acceleration tapers as $v_{eff}$ approaches its relativistic ceiling.

\section{Discussion}
FAE provides a structured theoretical model for atomic-gradient propulsion.
Although the required atomic field manipulation exceeds current technological
capabilities, the architecture offers a coherent mathematical basis for future
investigation. The relativistic constraint ensures physical consistency while
allowing continuous acceleration.

\section{Conclusion}
The First Atoms Engine defines a conceptual propulsion regime based on
atomic-gradient drift. Its simulation kernel, motion law, and field dynamics
form a unified theoretical architecture suitable for academic exploration.

